-- Encoding of the lambda-box (λ□) language in Arend.
--
-- Sources / inspiration:
--   * MetaRocq EAst.v   : erasure/theories/EAst.v   (the canonical λ□ `term`)
--   * lean-to-lambdabox : LeanToLambdaBox/Basic.lean (the `LBTerm` adaptation)
--   * Links
--     - https://github.com/peregrine-project/lean-to-lambdabox/blob/main/LeanToLambdaBox/Basic.lean
--     - https://metarocq.github.io/html/MetaRocq.Erasure.EAst.html
--     - https://metacoq.github.io/v1.2-8.16/MetaCoq.Erasure.EAst.html
--     - https://github.com/MetaRocq/metarocq/blob/coq-8.20/erasure/theories/EAst.v
-- We follow the LBTerm shape (cleaner names, locally-nameless friendly):
--   * keep `fvar` (a locally-nameless free variable, à la Lean) alongside `bvar`
--     (de Bruijn).  The canonical EAst λ□ uses only `bvar`; drop `fvar` if you
--     build de Bruijn directly during erasure.
--   * drop `tVar`, `tEvar`, `tCoFix`, `tLazy`, `tForce` (LBTerm does the same):
--     they are not needed for a strict, non-coinductive `\Set`+IO fragment.
--   * `prim` is simplified: MetaRocq/LBTerm carry 63-bit primitive ints; Arend
--     has no fixed-width BitVec, so we model the payload with the unbounded
--     Prelude `Int`.  This is the FIRST of three layers of the known int63
--     mismatch -- see the `PrimModel` comment below and KNOWN MISMATCH in
--     Compiler/ToJava.ard.

\import Data.Bool
\import Compiler.Int63 (int63Mod, int64Mod)
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String
-- All sequences are `Array` (Prelude) so ToJava can recurse inline via `xs i` and
-- there is no `List`/`Array` `nil`/`::` ambiguity. (No `\import Data.List`.)

-- When to use String, type synonym (ident) or class (FreeVarId) ?

\func Ident => String
\func DirPath => Array String

\class FreeVarId
  | varName : String

\data BinderName
  | named FreeVarId
  | anon  -- Identifiers that are allowed to be anonymous (i.e. "_" in concrete syntax).

\data ModPath
  | mpFile DirPath
  | mpBound DirPath Ident Nat
  | mpDot ModPath Ident

\record Kername (kerModPath : ModPath) (kerName : Ident)

\record InductiveId (indMutualBlock : Kername) (indIdx : Nat)
-- Q (Lean) : Which of the mutually inductive types defined in this block is used here?

\record ProjectionInfo
  (projIndType : InductiveId)
  (projParamCount : Nat)  -- number of (non-let) parameters
  (projFieldIdx : Nat)  -- argument to project

\record FixDef (term : \Type)
  | defName : BinderName
  | defBody : term
  | principalArgIdx : Nat  -- Principal/structural argument for recursion

-- THE KIND OF NUMBER IS PART OF THE TERM, not of the backend.
--
-- Upstream λ□ has one primitive integer: MetaRocq's `EPrimitive.v`
-- `primIntModel (i : PrimInt63.int)` with `prim_model_of primInt :=
-- PrimInt63.int`, the tag set coming from `common/theories/Primitive.v`
-- (`primInt63 | primFloat | primString | primArray` -- no primNat, no primZ);
-- the Rocq refman, "Primitive objects / Primitive integers"; and
-- lean-to-lambdabox's `PrimModel .primInt := BitVec 63`. `primInt63` IS that
-- tag, renamed so the width is part of the name rather than part of a comment.
--
-- Note this concerns ONLY `tPrim`: Rocq's default numbers (`nat`, `Z`) and
-- Lean's Peano mode erase to ordinary CONSTRUCTORS, never to `prim`.
--
-- WHY THE KIND BELONGS HERE. A backend may reasonably prefer JVM `long`
-- arithmetic, for speed. That choice cannot live in the backend: if the term
-- means i63 and the backend computes int64 then the backend is simply WRONG,
-- which this project proved -- and then papered over by parameterizing the
-- VALUE RELATION by the chosen normalization. A correctness statement whose
-- notion of "these two values correspond" takes a compiler flag cannot be read
-- without knowing the build configuration. With the kind in the term, each
-- backend is correct for the terms it is given, and choosing a representation
-- becomes a source-to-source rewrite that can be stated and argued about
-- instead of an invisible reinterpretation.
--
-- The precedent is in the frontend already: lean-to-lambdabox's `config.nat`
-- chooses between Peano constructors and machine i63 for Lean's `Nat`, and it
-- makes that choice by emitting DIFFERENT λ□ TERMS.
--
-- `primInt64` IS NOT YET COMPILED, deliberately. The operations are selected by
-- kername at a `const` node, which never sees its operands, so a kind on the
-- values cannot by itself pick `PRIM_ADD_LONG`; that needs per-kind axiom names
-- and is a separate step (see `Compiler/JavaAxioms.ard`). Declaring the kind first is
-- what lets the semantics talk about it at all.
--
-- DEVIATION FROM UPSTREAM, accepted: a `primInt64` term has no peregrine
-- counterpart, so `Compiler/Serialize.ard`'s export cannot represent one. λ□ is a
-- stepping stone here rather than an interchange format, and export is allowed
-- to be partial.
\data PrimTag
  | primInt63  -- MetaRocq/peregrine's `Uint63`: unsigned, cyclic mod 2^63
  | primInt64  -- two's-complement 64-bit, i.e. a JVM `long`
-- | primFloat
-- | primList

-- THE PAYLOAD IS BOUNDED BY CONSTRUCTION, which the old encoding was not. It
-- used the unbounded Prelude `Int`, so values >= 2^63 were representable and
-- nothing wrapped; this file called that "the FIRST of three layers of the
-- known int63 mismatch" and left it as FUTURE WORK ("model the payload as
-- `Fin (2^63)` / a `mod 2^63`-normalized Int").
--
-- DONE. The only way to build a payload is `Int63.wrap63`/`wrap64u`, which
-- normalize, so an out-of-range primitive cannot be written down -- and the
-- value relation compares exactly instead of normalizing as it goes.
\func PrimModel (t : PrimTag) : \Set0
  | primInt63 => Fin int63Mod
  | primInt64 => Fin int64Mod

\func PrimVal : \Type => \Sigma (t : PrimTag) (PrimModel t)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The Lambda Box Term
--  https://metarocq.github.io/html/MetaRocq.Erasure.EAst.html#term
--  https://github.com/peregrine-project/lean-to-lambdabox/blob/main/LeanToLambdaBox/Basic.lean#L90
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data LBTerm
  | box  -- represents computation irrelevant things, like proofs and types
  | bvar Nat  -- bound variable, accessed as a de Bruijn index
  | fvar FreeVarId  -- free variables (e.g. in a goal)
  -- tEvar  (not part of Lean pipeline?)
  | lambda BinderName LBTerm  -- \lam x => x + 1
  | letIn BinderName LBTerm LBTerm  -- \let | x => 1 \in x + 1
  | app LBTerm LBTerm  -- f x
  | const Kername  --  a constant living in the environment
  -- The array contains exactly all constructor fields (constructor-block form),
  -- as produced for external programs by Peregrine's `ast box` pass.
  | construct InductiveId Nat {- index of the constructor used -} (Array LBTerm)  -- \data ...  (Array so ToJava can recurse inline via `args i`)
  | case InductiveId Nat {- number of parameters -} LBTerm (Array (\Sigma (Array BinderName) LBTerm))
  -- \case {Type, number of parameters} expr \with ...
  | proj ProjectionInfo LBTerm
  | fix (Array (FixDef LBTerm)) Nat {- index of the one mutually defined function we want to access -}
  -- | tCoFix  (not part of Lean pipeline?)
  | prim PrimVal
-- | lazy  -- lazy evaltiation? (not part of Lean pipeline?)
-- | force  -- eager evaltiation? (not part of Lean pipeline?)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The Lambda Box Program
--  https://metarocq.github.io/html/MetaRocq.Erasure.EAst.html#program
--  https://github.com/peregrine-project/lean-to-lambdabox/blob/main/LeanToLambdaBox/Basic.lean#L182
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\record ConstructorBody (cstrName : Ident) (cstrNargs : Nat)

\record ProjectionBody (projBodyName : Ident)

\data AllowedEliminations  -- TODO : adjust for Arend?
  | IntoSProp
  | IntoPropSProp
  | IntoSetPropSProp
  | IntoAny

\record OneInductiveBody
  (indName : Ident)
  (indPropositional : Bool)   -- Should always be false? since erasure should remove anything which ends up in Prop
  (indKelim : AllowedEliminations)
  (indCtors : Array ConstructorBody)
  (indProjs : Array ProjectionBody)  -- This is only about giving user-visible names to projections, but `peregrine` complains about wellformedness if it is empty.

\data RecursivityKind
  | finite
  | cofinite  -- Lean doesn't have primitive coinductive types, but including this here anyway to match the MetaCoq side.
  | bifinite

\record MutualInductiveBody
  (indFinite : RecursivityKind)  -- Should always be finite ?
  (indNpars : Nat)  -- Number of parameters to this family of mutually inductive types.
  (indBodies : Array OneInductiveBody)

\record ConstantBody (cstBody : Maybe LBTerm)

\data GlobalDecl
  | constantDecl ConstantBody
  | inductiveDecl MutualInductiveBody

-- ORDER: the list is newest-first, i.e. the HEAD is the declaration added
-- LAST. MetaRocq's `EWellformed.wf_glob` (and peregrine's `CheckWf.v`) checks
-- each entry's body against only the *tail* of the list (`wf_global_decl Σ d`
-- for `(kn,d)::Σ`), so a declaration may only reference declarations that come
-- AFTER it -- dependents first, dependencies last, the reverse of a normal
-- "declare before use" order. Lookup (`lookup_env`) scans from the head, so
-- getting this wrong is invisible to our own generator and only shows up when
-- an exported program is checked by peregrine.
\func GlobalDeclarations : \Type => Array (\Sigma Kername GlobalDecl)

\func LBProgram : \Type => \Sigma GlobalDeclarations LBTerm


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The rest of Basic.lean, not otherwise needed by Compiler/ToJava.ard, but needed by
-- Compiler/Serialize.ard (mirrors the tail of Basic.lean: ASTType/Program plus the
-- extraction-attribute-remapping types).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Basic.lean's `Program := ASTType`; `term` is `Option LBTerm` there (unlike
-- LBProgram above, which requires one) -- a program can be a bare library
-- with no distinguished entry term.
\data ASTType
  | untyped GlobalDeclarations (Maybe LBTerm)

\record RemappedInductive
  (reIndName : Ident)
  (reIndCtors : Array Ident)
  (reIndMatch : Maybe Ident)

\record ExtractInductive
  (extractCstrs : Array Kername)
  (extractElim : Kername)

\record RemappedConstant
  (reConstExt : Maybe Ident)
  (reConstArity : Nat)
  (reConstGC : Bool)
  (reConstInl : Bool)
  (reConstS : Ident)

\data RemapInductive
  | knIndRemap ExtractInductive
  | stringIndRemap RemappedInductive

\record InductiveMapping
  (indMapKn : Kername)
  (indMapS : Ident)
  (indMapN : Array Nat)

\record AttributesConfig
  (acInlinings : Array Kername)
  (acConstRemappings : Array (\Sigma Kername RemappedConstant))
  (acIndRemappings : Array (\Sigma InductiveId RemapInductive))
  (acCstrReorders : Array InductiveMapping)
  (acCustomAttributes : Array (\Sigma Kername Ident))
